  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Division List",
    "overview": "Tabled sets Parliament's own record, the printed division list from the Journals of the House, as a readable page. Every object is a record with a count: names in ruled columns, figures in tabular numerals, and totals set large at the foot of the column under a double rule. The page is warm paper with deep ink in light mode and a warm near-black ground with paper-coloured ink in dark mode. Depth comes from rules, never from cards or shadows.\n\nDensity is true density. Tables and lists run on a strict hairline system with no whitespace inflation; the generosity is spent on the reading column (66ch) and on the large serif figures that answer the reader's question first. One accent, House of Commons green, carries links, focus, the current-stage flag and the active nav rule. Colour otherwise lives only in data marks: party swatches, hemicycle seats and the neutral ink and sepia ballot marks.\n\nThe system refuses the SaaS dashboard: no cards, no KPI tiles, no gradient charts, no tinted panels per party.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Warm paper and ink, light and dark, both tuned around hue 62 to 84.",
      "Hairline rules for every division; a strong rule closes page headers; a double rule sits above every total.",
      "Brygada 1918 for titles, figures and totals; Libre Franklin for everything read or operated; tabular lining figures everywhere.",
      "Member names set in small caps, as a division list sets them.",
      "State drawn by form (fill, ring, dash, strike, flag), so every chart reads without colour."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Data Mark Rule",
        "body": "Party colour appears only in marks no larger than a swatch or a seat. Text, backgrounds, borders and page themes never take a party hue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Neutral Ballot Rule",
        "body": "Yea and nay are ink and sepia, never green and red. Neither side of a division gets a positive colour.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "Commons Green is the only chromatic UI colour; if it is not a link, focus, selection or the current-stage flag, it is not green.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "Body sets tabular lining figures globally; every count lines up in its column.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Small Caps Name Rule",
        "body": "Every member name in running UI (lists, ballot tables, sponsors, dissent lists) uses small caps with capitals at full height (0.02em tracking). Page-title names stay in the display face.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "body": "All labels, headings and buttons are sentence case. No uppercase kickers or eyebrows. The only uppercase is postal-code input, which formats the data.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ruled Page Rule",
        "body": "If a group needs separating, draw a hairline. Cards, tiles and shadows on in-flow content are not part of this world.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Double Rule Rule",
        "body": "A total is set under a 3px double strong rule, as on a printed division list. Nothing but a total gets a double rule.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do separate with hairlines (rule) and close page headers with the strong rule.",
      "Do set every total in the serif under the 3px double strong rule.",
      "Do order parties by seat count in every table, legend and hemicycle.",
      "Do set member names in small caps (`name-sc`) wherever they appear in lists, tables and sponsor lines.",
      "Do draw state by form (fill, ring, dash, strike, flag) so charts read in greyscale, and pair every chart with a text alternative.",
      "Do label every zone with its literal plain-English name in sentence case.",
      "Do keep prose to the 66ch measure and figures tabular."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use party colour outside swatches, legend dots and hemicycle seats.",
      "Don't colour yea and nay as positive and negative; use the ink and sepia ballot marks.",
      "Don't use cards, KPI tiles, gradient charts or shadows on in-flow content.",
      "Don't add uppercase kickers or eyebrows above headings.",
      "Don't use em dashes in Tabled's own copy. Official riding names keep their official punctuation as data.",
      "Don't flag anything but the current stage of a bill.",
      "Don't use Commons Green for anything other than links, focus, selection and the current-stage flag."
    ]
  }
